Ladybird learns which sites challenge a spoofed User-Agent and sends
its own to them from then on, by retrying a navigation that a Cloudflare
challenge page answered, and persisting the site as a force_Ladybird
site-compatibility rule in the profile. No other engine adapts a
User-Agent to a challenge: none of them spoofs one, so none is ever
challenged for the name it gives. The nearest relative is Firefox’s
static per-site override list in its bundled webcompat add-on
(browser/extensions/webcompat/data/interventions/*.json, applied by the
onBeforeSendHeaders listener in lib/interventions.js and the content
scripts under injections/js/), which adds a Chrome token to look like
Chrome on a site that blocks Firefox — the inverse of what Ladybird
does here — and is hand-curated and shipped through Remote Settings
rather than learned. WebKit’s UserAgentQuirks::quirksForURL() (in the
GLib port) is the same kind of static list, and Chromium hands its
embedder WebContents::SetUserAgentOverride() and ships no list.
